00:02:27,117 --> 00:02:27,717
[SPEAKER_02]: this evening.

41
00:02:29,438 --> 00:02:33,301
[SPEAKER_02]: So thank you very much, counselors for having us back here.

42
00:02:34,721 --> 00:02:37,683
[SPEAKER_02]: So most of you are familiar with the housing production plan.

43
00:02:38,063 --> 00:02:46,788
[SPEAKER_02]: This is a document that is codified in state law that they ask communities to have to talk about how you make housing production.

44
00:02:46,868 --> 00:02:50,590
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a tool that is used throughout the state for various purposes.

45
00:02:52,273 --> 00:02:56,838
[SPEAKER_02]: This the housing production plan was actually prepared for us by a consultant.

46
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:03,585
[SPEAKER_02]: And in this plan, there are lists housing goals and strategies.

47
00:03:04,045 --> 00:03:14,215
[SPEAKER_02]: There's information about the city's demographic profile, the current housing conditions, development constraints for our city, and implementation capacity and resources.

48
00:03:14,756 --> 00:03:15,557
[SPEAKER_02]: If you are somebody

49
00:03:15,993 --> 00:03:23,960
[SPEAKER_02]: watching from the public who is really interested in housing issues, this is a great document to get a lot of background from what's going on in the city.

50
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:37,292
[SPEAKER_02]: The counselors have all had it before I know that I'm two of the counselors are new that Daniel actually spoke with counselor Collins today to bring her up to speed and and offered that as well to counselor saying.

51
00:03:38,256 --> 00:03:43,897
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm sorry so the goals in Medford's 5 in.

52
00:03:44,637 --> 00:03:50,539
[SPEAKER_02]: So housing production plans are designed to be 5 year plans and things that you want to accomplish within 5 years.

53
00:03:51,059 --> 00:03:56,660
[SPEAKER_02]: So our goals of this plan to address local housing needs meet production goals.

54
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:04,021
[SPEAKER_02]: And actually should clarify that the state has a goal for all communities that 10% of your housing.

55
00:04:04,041 --> 00:04:07,362
[SPEAKER_02]: The deed restricted affordable housing.

56
00:04:07,762 --> 00:04:16,544
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's not just housing that's affordable by the dollars that it costs to rent it, but that they're restricted to people who are income eligible.

57
00:04:16,564 --> 00:04:24,566
[SPEAKER_02]: So the goal would be to meet that production goal of the state that's kind of a requirement of the state document.

58
00:04:25,246 --> 00:04:33,188
[SPEAKER_02]: Promote a welcoming, diverse, intergenerational, and inclusive city to foster safe, well-designed, and sustainable housing.

59
00:04:34,073 --> 00:04:49,383
[SPEAKER_02]: integrate affordable and diverse housing options throughout the city, preserve the affordability of existing affordable homes, expand local capacity to implement housing initiatives, and to promote transparency and engagement.

60
00:04:50,224 --> 00:04:53,326
[SPEAKER_02]: And to accomplish these, there are 18 strategies.

61
00:04:53,826 --> 00:04:55,327
[SPEAKER_02]: I am not going to read all 18 strategies,

62
00:04:56,915 --> 00:05:03,760
[SPEAKER_02]: in the document there is a page that lists them and indicates which goals each of these strategies is designed to meet.

63
00:05:04,981 --> 00:05:06,262
[SPEAKER_02]: All of these are strategies.

64
00:05:06,422 --> 00:05:15,088
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a menu of things that we can choose from with the goal of helping us get to that 10% deed restricted affordable housing here in Medford.

65
00:05:16,169 --> 00:05:19,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Many of these are things that actually would take council action

66
00:05:20,294 --> 00:05:29,202
[SPEAKER_02]: To then make happen meant most of them all of them have to be sort of researched and developed by administrative staff in the city housing staff.

67
00:05:30,123 --> 00:05:38,471
[SPEAKER_02]: But a lot of them anything that has to do with zoning would then need to go to the city council to be approved.

68
00:05:39,532 --> 00:05:45,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Some of these things continue to city recruitment of mission based developers that's you know, obviously us working on that.

69
00:05:46,561 --> 00:05:53,044
[SPEAKER_02]: So when we were in front of the city council, there was a lot of concern about two of the strategies.

70
00:05:53,905 --> 00:05:59,428
[SPEAKER_02]: And so what we have here this evening are our proposed language changes for those two strategies.

71
00:06:00,628 --> 00:06:04,850
[SPEAKER_02]: The first was adopt affordable infill zoning provisions.

72
00:06:05,631 --> 00:06:11,894
[SPEAKER_02]: And our proposed change is to research the feasibility of affordable infill zoning provisions.

73
00:06:12,875 --> 00:06:15,596
[SPEAKER_02]: The other that the council had concerns with was

74
00:06:16,218 --> 00:06:19,761
[SPEAKER_02]: foster affordable infill on city-owned vacant lots.

75
00:06:20,622 --> 00:06:27,988
[SPEAKER_02]: And our proposal is to change it to consider affordable infill on city-owned vacant lots where appropriate.

76
00:06:29,189 --> 00:06:34,094
[SPEAKER_02]: And I just wanted for a minute to use this to illustrate what it is we're talking about here.

77
00:06:34,114 --> 00:06:36,676
[SPEAKER_02]: Because most people, particularly with the city-owned lots,

78
00:06:37,109 --> 00:06:39,531
[SPEAKER_02]: Think about these giant parking lots out here.

79
00:06:40,052 --> 00:06:43,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are things that we're looking at requests for proposals from developers.

80
00:06:43,936 --> 00:06:45,497
[SPEAKER_02]: We're looking at large projects.

81
00:06:46,018 --> 00:06:48,781
[SPEAKER_02]: This infill provision is this.

82
00:06:48,881 --> 00:06:50,362
[SPEAKER_02]: So this is an example.

83
00:06:50,823 --> 00:06:55,607
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a relatively random clip from our assessors maps.

84
00:06:56,448 --> 00:07:00,731
[SPEAKER_02]: And in some of these lots, so the numbers are the lot numbers.

85
00:07:00,911 --> 00:07:03,533
[SPEAKER_02]: They're not the house numbers, but there's the assessor's lots numbers.

86
00:07:04,113 --> 00:07:06,775
[SPEAKER_02]: In a few of them, this one here, you can see the number 4,500.

87
00:07:07,055 --> 00:07:10,477
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the square footage of that property.

88
00:07:10,978 --> 00:07:11,698
[SPEAKER_02]: Here you can see 4,950.

89
00:07:11,798 --> 00:07:12,739
[SPEAKER_02]: Here you can see 4,770.

90
00:07:12,819 --> 00:07:15,761
[SPEAKER_02]: And these lots all to the eye look like they're about the same size.

91
00:07:15,781 --> 00:07:16,281
[SPEAKER_02]: These lots were made

92
00:07:24,727 --> 00:07:26,328
[SPEAKER_02]: before the 1960s.

93
00:07:26,508 --> 00:07:28,249
[SPEAKER_02]: They were made a very long time ago.

94
00:07:28,309 --> 00:07:31,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Many of these lots were divided up in the early 1900s.

95
00:07:32,911 --> 00:07:43,237
[SPEAKER_02]: In the 60s, the city came through with zoning and said that for a single family house in Medford, in most of the city, you need a 5,000 square foot lot.

96
00:07:43,817 --> 00:07:48,400
[SPEAKER_02]: And there are actually some areas where you need a 7,000 square foot lot.

97
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:52,602
[SPEAKER_02]: So not one of these lots with the square footage on it,

98
00:07:53,863 --> 00:07:56,204
[SPEAKER_02]: is developable under our current zoning.

99
00:07:56,804 --> 00:08:02,007
[SPEAKER_02]: So that makes this lot with the 4,950 would have to go to the Zoning Board of Appeals.

100
00:08:02,607 --> 00:08:07,429
[SPEAKER_02]: And so would these other two lots down here in order to build a new house on them.

101
00:08:07,850 --> 00:08:13,893
[SPEAKER_02]: The current houses, they could be torn down and rebuilt, but to do a new one, you'd have to go to Zoning Board.

102
00:08:15,073 --> 00:08:23,117
[SPEAKER_02]: So our proposal is that we should look at some zoning that talks about if your lot is between

103
00:08:23,618 --> 00:08:36,502
[SPEAKER_02]: four and 5,000 square feet, and you agreed to put an affordable deed restriction on the property, you don't have to get a zoning variance to build on that property for the size of the lot.

104
00:08:37,042 --> 00:08:47,005
[SPEAKER_02]: If you wanted to ignore some of our other zoning, like how far you are from the side lot or the front, you would still have to go to the zoning board for a variance for those things.

105
00:08:47,472 --> 00:08:49,493
[SPEAKER_02]: it would simply be about the size of the lot.

106
00:08:49,974 --> 00:08:55,498
[SPEAKER_02]: And what would happen is it would unlock the development potential of these existing properties.

107
00:08:55,878 --> 00:09:02,803
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you might walk by, an average person would look by a reasonable person and say, why can't you develop that lot?

108
00:09:02,843 --> 00:09:04,604
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just like all the others next to it.

109
00:09:05,024 --> 00:09:12,509
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because our zoning came in and took away the rights of those property owners back in the 1960s and said, you can't develop them anymore, they're too small.

110
00:09:13,230 --> 00:09:15,091
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what infill provisions are.

111
00:09:15,552 --> 00:09:20,936
[SPEAKER_02]: So we are not with this housing production plan saying we should pass this and allow it.

112
00:09:21,356 --> 00:09:23,498
[SPEAKER_02]: What we're saying is we should study it.

113
00:09:23,538 --> 00:09:30,222
[SPEAKER_02]: We should consider it in order to make that change in zoning and allow this infill development.

114
00:09:30,562 --> 00:09:35,526
[SPEAKER_02]: We would actually have to come to city council and propose a zoning change to the city council to do that.

115
00:09:36,046 --> 00:09:44,212
[SPEAKER_02]: But we do think that this would incentivize people to build single family homes that are affordable deed restricted.

116
00:09:45,635 --> 00:09:49,656
[SPEAKER_02]: because otherwise these lots are not, you can't develop on them.

117
00:09:49,757 --> 00:09:50,837
[SPEAKER_02]: They're just large yards.

118
00:09:51,617 --> 00:09:57,439
[SPEAKER_02]: So that is sort of our proposal for how to change the items that we heard concerns about in the past.

119
00:09:58,499 --> 00:10:07,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Our proposed next steps is that the city council would vote the housing production plan with these amendments out of the committee tonight.

120
00:10:08,502 --> 00:10:13,524
[SPEAKER_02]: We will then submit a clean revised copy of the housing production plan with these changes.

121
00:10:14,543 --> 00:10:16,604
[SPEAKER_02]: and then to the City Council.

122
00:10:17,104 --> 00:10:20,526
[SPEAKER_02]: The City Council would vote to adopt the revised HPP.

123
00:10:20,966 --> 00:10:25,348
[SPEAKER_02]: And just to be clear, we would then need to send it to the state for their approval.

124
00:10:25,869 --> 00:10:38,615
[SPEAKER_02]: Once they receive the version that has been approved by both the Community Development Board and the City Council, then they would review it and they would certify it back to the date that they reviewed it, unless they ask for changes.

125
00:10:38,975 --> 00:10:40,636
[SPEAKER_02]: We don't expect them to ask for changes.

126
00:10:40,716 --> 00:10:42,837
[SPEAKER_02]: They did review this about a year ago.

127
00:10:44,834 --> 00:10:48,195
[SPEAKER_02]: and we addressed those before we ever brought it to the council the first time.

128
